"1. Lancet Infect Dis. 2016 Dec;16(12):e288-e303. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(16)30402-9. Epub 2016 Nov 2.\n\nNew WHO recommendations on intraoperative and postoperative measures for \nsurgical site infection prevention: an evidence-based global perspective.\n\nAllegranzi B(1), Zayed B(2), Bischoff P(3), Kubilay NZ(2), de Jonge S(4), de \nVries F(4), Gomes SM(5), Gans S(4), Wallert ED(4), Wu X(6), Abbas M(7), \nBoermeester MA(4), Dellinger EP(8), Egger M(9), Gastmeier P(3), Guirao X(10), \nRen J(6), Pittet D(11), Solomkin JS(12); WHO Guidelines Development Group.\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Infection Prevention and Control Global Unit, Service Delivery and Safety, \nWHO, Geneva, Switzerland. Electronic address: allegranzib@who.int.\n(2)Infection Prevention and Control Global Unit, Service Delivery and Safety, \nWHO, Geneva, Switzerland.\n(3)Institute of Hygiene and Environmental Medicine, Charit\u00e9-University Medicine, \nBerlin, Germany.\n(4)Department of Surgery, Academic Medical Center Amsterdam, Amsterdam, \nNetherlands.\n(5)OASIS Global, Cincinnati, OH, USA.\n(6)Jinling Hospital, Medical School of Nanjing University, Nanjing, Jiangsu, \nChina.\n(7)Infection Control Programme, University of Geneva Hospitals and Faculty of \nMedicine, Geneva, Switzerland.\n(8)Department of Surgery, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA.\n(9)Institute of Social and Preventive Medicine, University of Bern, Bern, \nSwitzerland.\n(10)Parc Taul\u00ed Hospital Universitari, Barcelona, Spain.\n(11)Infection Control Programme, University of Geneva Hospitals and Faculty of \nMedicine, Geneva, Switzerland; WHO Collaborating Centre on Patient Safety \n(Infection Control and Improving Practices), University of Geneva Hospitals and \nFaculty of Medicine, Geneva, Switzerland.\n(12)OASIS Global, Cincinnati, OH, USA; University of Cincinnati College of \nMedicine, Cincinnati, OH, USA.\n\nComment in\n    Lancet Infect Dis. 2017 Mar;17(3):260-261. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(17)30078-6.\n    Lancet Infect Dis. 2017 Mar;17(3):261-262. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(17)30079-8.\n    Lancet Infect Dis. 2017 Mar;17(3):262-264. doi: \n10.1016/S1473-3099(17)30081-6.\n    Lancet Infect Dis. 2017 Mar;17(3):262. doi: 10.1016/S1473-3099(17)30080-4.\n    Rev Chilena Infectol. 2017 Oct;34(5):523-524. doi: \n10.4067/S0716-10182017000500523.\n\nSurgical site infections (SSIs) are the most common health-care-associated \ninfections in developing countries, but they also represent a substantial \nepidemiological burden in high-income countries. The prevention of these \ninfections is complex and requires the integration of a range of preventive \nmeasures before, during, and after surgery. No international guidelines are \navailable and inconsistencies in the interpretation of evidence and \nrecommendations in national guidelines have been identified. Considering the \nprevention of SSIs as a priority for patient safety, WHO has developed \nevidence-based and expert consensus-based recommendations on the basis of an \nextensive list of preventive measures. We present in this Review 16 \nrecommendations specific to the intraoperative and postoperative periods. The \nWHO recommendations were developed with a global perspective and they take into \naccount the balance between benefits and harms, the evidence quality level, cost \nand resource use implications, and patient values and preferences.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2016 World Health Organization. Published by Elsevier Ltd/Inc/BV. \nAll rights reserved. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1016/S1473-3099(16)30402-9\nPMID: 27816414 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"